Bounds checks for lzokay's decompressor.

Applied by build_lzo_shim (native/build-lzo-shim.sh) to the lzokay.cpp of the commit
pinned in native/build-config.sh. It applies with no offset; if a new pin makes it
fail, check whether upstream has fixed these and rework or drop the patch.

lzokay::decompress is handed data from untrusted archives, and as published it trusts
that data in two places:

1. CONSUME_ZERO_BYTE_LENGTH skips a run of zero bytes without looking for the end of
   the input. Whenever the input ends inside such a run it reads on past the buffer,
   until it meets a non-zero byte or unmapped memory.

2. NEEDS_IN and NEEDS_OUT compare "pointer + count" with the end of the buffer. A
   length can be up to 255 times the number of zero bytes in front of it, so where
   pointers are 32 bits wide a few megabytes of zeros make "pointer + count" wrap
   around and pass the check. The copy that follows then runs far outside both the
   input and the output buffer.

The checks below compare the count with the space that is left instead, which cannot
wrap, and stop the zero-byte scan at the end of the input. The compressor uses
NEEDS_OUT as well and is checked the same way.

--- a/lzokay.cpp
+++ b/lzokay.cpp
@@ -40,3 +40,3 @@
 #define NEEDS_IN(count) \
-  if (inp + (count) > inp_end) { \
+  if (std::size_t(inp_end - inp) < std::size_t(count)) { \
     dst_size = outp - dst; \
@@ -46,3 +46,3 @@
 #define NEEDS_OUT(count) \
-  if (outp + (count) > outp_end) { \
+  if (std::size_t(outp_end - outp) < std::size_t(count)) { \
     dst_size = outp - dst; \
@@ -55,3 +55,3 @@
     const uint8_t *old_inp = inp; \
-    while (*inp == 0) ++inp; \
+    while (inp < inp_end && *inp == 0) ++inp; \
     offset = inp - old_inp; \
